## Scope and limitations

Aspose.PDF FOSS for Go is a pure-Go library covering document assembly, content
authoring, forms, annotations, security, conversion, and rendering — with no CGo
or native dependencies. A few boundaries are worth noting: `Validate()` checks
structural integrity only (parseable and internally consistent); it does not
check PDF/A or PDF/UA conformance — use `ValidatePDFA`/`ValidatePDFUA` for those.
`Document.VerifySignatures` confirms cryptographic validity, integrity, and
whole-document coverage of a signature, but trust-chain validation against a
certificate authority is the caller's responsibility. The built-in renderer does
not support mesh shadings (PDF shading types 4–7), and `ConvertToPDFA` cannot
embed `Symbol`/`ZapfDingbats` or composite (CJK) fonts. PDF → Markdown export
does not reconstruct tables: cell text flows as plain paragraphs rather than
GFM table syntax (the Markdown → PDF direction, by contrast, renders GFM
tables fully via the table engine).

For the full Aspose.PDF feature set, cloud APIs, and additional language
bindings, see [Aspose.PDF Enterprise Edition](https://products.aspose.com/pdf/).

## Development and testing

```bash
# Run all tests
go test ./...

# Run a single test
go test -run TestDocumentSplit ./...

# Run tests with verbose output
go test -v ./...

# Build (no binary — library only)
go build ./...
```

Standalone runnable examples live in `_examples/<name>/main.go` (run individually,
e.g. `go run ./_examples/feature_showcase`); short focused `ExampleXxx` functions
in `examples_test.go` are validated by `go test` via their `// Output:` comments.

